   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the civil rights law, in relation to personnel records of police officers, firefighters and correction officers",
    "basePrintNo": "S4818",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4818-2013",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §50-a, Civ Rts L",
    "lawSection": "Civil Rights Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4818\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the civil rights law, in relation to\npersonnel records of police officers, firefighters and correction\nofficers\n\nPURPOSE:  This bill would expand the rights of employees whose\npersonnel information is considered confidential and not subject to\ninspection or review unless when mandated by lawful court order.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  This bill amends subdivisions 1 and 4 of\nsection 50-a of the civil rights law\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  The information used to evaluate performance toward\ncontinued employment or promotion, as well as the information\ncontained therein should be released upon receipt of a request,\nprovided that such request is accompanied by a written authorization\nsigned by the subject employee authorizing the release of such record.\n\nIn 2006, at least 35 NYPD police officers had been chosen for\nemployment with the Port Authority Police Department (\"PAPD\"). As part\nof the PAPD's background investigation into the applicant police\nofficers, the PAPD sought employment and personnel records from the\nNYPD. The police officers then signed for their records to be released\nto the PAPD.\n\nHowever, the NYPD refused to release the records, and refused to allow\nthe PAPD investigators to review the personnel records at NYPD\nHeadquarters. The NYPD would release only an \"abstract,\" providing\nname, last job title, dates of employment, and if separated, reason\nfor separation. As a result of the NYPD's refusal to provide the\nrecords, the PAPD deferred the police officers' applications. In 2006,\nthe PBA organized and financed an Article 78 proceeding on behalf of\nthe police officer PAPD applicants challenging the NYPD's policy of\nrefusing to release the employment records or grant access to such\nrecords. The police officers prevailed on their request for a\npreliminary injunction in aid of the Article 78; however, the\ninjunction was reversed by the First Department Appellate Division on\nthe basis that the NYPD's policy of restricting access to personnel\nrecords is well within the Police Commissioner's discretion and broad\npowers granted by the New York City Charter. Further, the court found\nthat the police officers' reliance on Civil Rights Law § 50-a was\nmisplaced, since \"this law,...does not require the release of\npersonnel records to third parties and in any event, the statute does\nnot create a private right of action for police officers for a claimed\nviolation of Civil Rights Law § 50-a.\" As a result of the First\nDepartment's decision, the police officers were not hired by the PAPD.\n\nThese officers should have the right to access their own personal\nrecords and give written authorization for another entity to also\naccess those files.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:  New Bill\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:  See the bill.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:  This act shall take effect immediately.\n\n\n",
